      "problem": "Given $f(x)=8{x}^{2}+1$ and $g(x)=2x-2,$ find  $(fg)(1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =8(1)^{2}+1=9 \\\\ g(1) & =2(1)-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=9\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(8{x}^{2}+1)(2x-2) \\\\  & =16{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =16(1)^{3}-16(1)^{2}+2(1)-2 \\\\  & =16-16+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
