      "problem": "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}-4$ and $g(x)=2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =(2)^{2}-4=0 \\\\ g(2) & =2(2)+2=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(2)=\\frac{f(2)}{g(2)}=\\frac{0}{6}=0\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(2):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(2) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}-4}{2(2)+2}=\\frac{0}{6}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
